2026-10-04 01:36:51 | INFO | P06-CO2 | CUSTOM OBSERVATIONS:
    I used GDP per person to predict annual CO2 emissions per person.

    I created a new gdp_per_capita feature by dividing GDP by population.
    This allowed me to compare economic activity and emissions without
    total population size dominating the analysis.

    The baseline RMSE was 5.61.
    The LinearRegression RMSE was 3.09.

    Compared with the baseline, the LinearRegression model reduced
    the typical prediction error by 2.52, or about 45%.

    The model R-squared was 0.693, meaning GDP per person accounted
    for about 69% of the variation in CO2 emissions per person in
    the held-out test observations.

    In the residual plot, I observed that residuals were generally
    centered around zero, but their spread was larger at higher GDP
    per-person values. Some predictions still had relatively large errors.

    Based on this evidence, I conclude that GDP per person is a useful
    predictor of CO2 emissions per person for this dataset, but it does
    not fully explain emissions and should not be interpreted as proving
    that GDP causes CO2 emissions.

    Next, I would like to investigate whether energy-source variables,
    such as coal, oil, or gas CO2, improve prediction accuracy.

